WEBVTT

00:03.200 --> 00:05.440
It strikes me
that this is now one

00:05.440 --> 00:09.420
of the great modern forensic
mysteries, isn't it?

00:09.420 --> 00:12.320
Will we ever really know?

00:12.320 --> 00:15.680
I would maintain that
the suicide scenario

00:15.680 --> 00:18.640
is disproven by the
impossibility of the injury

00:18.640 --> 00:21.000
pattern that we see
in the decedent,

00:21.000 --> 00:22.940
and that only leaves homicide.

00:22.940 --> 00:26.060
That's Dr. Michael
Freeman, a forensic expert,

00:26.060 --> 00:28.920
talking about his
months-long study of Jeffrey

00:28.920 --> 00:30.680
Epstein's autopsy.

00:30.680 --> 00:33.760
Epstein died over seven
years ago in a prison cell

00:33.760 --> 00:35.740
in this building
right behind me,

00:35.740 --> 00:38.520
under a cloud of
suspicion and confusion.

00:38.520 --> 00:41.160
His death was ruled a
clear-cut case of suicide

00:41.160 --> 00:44.340
by hanging by the New York
City medical examiner.

00:44.340 --> 00:47.600
But to say that doubts persist
would be an understatement.

00:47.600 --> 00:49.660
In fact, across the
political spectrum,

00:49.660 --> 00:52.540
most Americans believe,
without evidence,

00:52.540 --> 00:54.400
that Epstein was murdered.

00:54.400 --> 00:58.480
Well, now they may have some
because for the first time,

00:58.480 --> 01:02.780
an independent scientific
reanalysis of the autopsy

01:02.780 --> 01:03.530
is being released.

01:03.530 --> 01:04.947
MICHAEL FREEMAN:
The injuries that

01:04.947 --> 01:07.340
were documented by the
medical examiner were not

01:07.340 --> 01:09.980
consistent with a
suicidal hanging,

01:09.980 --> 01:14.180
and the injuries that were
attributed to something other

01:14.180 --> 01:17.620
than potentially a physical
struggle or assault were

01:17.620 --> 01:18.860
just overlooked.

01:18.860 --> 01:21.240
DAVID SCOTT: Freeman is
not a forensic pathologist,

01:21.240 --> 01:24.680
but he's a medical doctor and a
professor of forensic medicine,

01:24.680 --> 01:26.620
and he's testified
as a forensic expert

01:26.620 --> 01:28.940
in many high-profile
court cases.

01:28.940 --> 01:31.620
We asked him to review
the autopsy photos

01:31.620 --> 01:34.900
that Jeffrey Epstein's brother,
Mark Epstein, provided us.

01:34.900 --> 01:38.420
Mark Epstein then shared more
never-before publicly released

01:38.420 --> 01:42.260
photos from his brother's
autopsy for Freeman's report.

01:42.260 --> 01:45.083
We warn you, the images
are very graphic.

01:45.083 --> 01:46.500
MICHAEL FREEMAN:
So the photograph

01:46.500 --> 01:48.860
that you see in front of
you now is the photograph

01:48.860 --> 01:55.180
that I was contacted by CBS News
and asked to give comment about.

01:55.180 --> 01:59.020
And when I saw this
photograph, my first reaction

01:59.020 --> 02:04.410
was that that is a very
unusual looking injury

02:04.410 --> 02:06.570
for a hanging on the neck.

02:06.570 --> 02:09.770
But first, there's some
things you need to know.

02:09.770 --> 02:13.030
Forensic pathology is
not an exact science.

02:13.030 --> 02:15.810
Credible experts often
interpret the same evidence

02:15.810 --> 02:18.130
very differently,
and neck injuries

02:18.130 --> 02:22.490
from hanging or strangulation
can be especially ambiguous.

02:22.490 --> 02:25.690
Add to that the death
investigation of Jeffrey Epstein

02:25.690 --> 02:29.290
was stymied by a cascade of
blunders, which limit what can

02:29.290 --> 02:31.270
ever be known with certainty.

02:31.270 --> 02:33.690
The scene of Epstein's
death was never

02:33.690 --> 02:36.150
sealed for forensic
investigation.

02:36.150 --> 02:39.430
And evidence was never
properly preserved and tested,

02:39.430 --> 02:41.090
according to our reporting.

02:41.090 --> 02:44.770
The body itself was moved before
any forensic study or even

02:44.770 --> 02:46.530
photographs were taken.

02:46.530 --> 02:48.770
Most of the
closed-circuit cameras

02:48.770 --> 02:51.610
in Epstein's wing of the
prison at the time of his death

02:51.610 --> 02:53.210
were not recording.

02:53.210 --> 02:56.250
And it took two years
for federal investigators

02:56.250 --> 02:58.890
to interview the
corrections officer, who

02:58.890 --> 03:01.690
says he found Epstein
hanging in his cell.

03:01.690 --> 03:03.790
That man, Michael
Thomas, had been

03:03.790 --> 03:06.810
charged with lying and
falsifying prison records

03:06.810 --> 03:09.990
and provided a sworn statement
to the Department of Justice

03:09.990 --> 03:12.110
in exchange for leniency.

03:12.110 --> 03:15.670
It took another year after that
for those same investigators

03:15.670 --> 03:18.390
to talk to the New York
City medical examiner who

03:18.390 --> 03:21.710
studied Epstein's body
and ruled it a suicide.

03:21.710 --> 03:24.070
The Department of Justice
finally released a report

03:24.070 --> 03:27.930
in 2023, four years
after Epstein's death,

03:27.930 --> 03:31.590
affirming the suicide,
but leaving, for critics,

03:31.590 --> 03:33.570
many unanswered questions.

03:33.570 --> 03:36.270
Now, Dr. Michael
Freeman enters the fray

03:36.270 --> 03:39.510
with a new critique of the New
York City medical examiner's

03:39.510 --> 03:42.870
suicide findings and a new
analysis of photographs,

03:42.870 --> 03:45.950
he says, clearly
indicates homicide.

03:45.950 --> 03:47.183
This was completely unique.

03:47.183 --> 03:49.350
I had never seen an
investigation of a hanging death

03:49.350 --> 03:54.270
like this because in all hanging
death investigations that you

03:54.270 --> 03:57.550
see in the literature,
or that I've ever

03:57.550 --> 04:02.310
been exposed to as a
investigator or as an expert,

04:02.310 --> 04:05.680
there were a number
of missing factors.

04:05.680 --> 04:10.060
There were no photographs
of the decedent hanging,

04:10.060 --> 04:13.475
where somebody found some
time after they'd been dead,

04:13.475 --> 04:15.100
a picture will be
taken of them hanging

04:15.100 --> 04:18.140
in the cell with the
ligature in place.

04:18.140 --> 04:22.140
There were no description
of the ligature in place

04:22.140 --> 04:24.780
after the body had
been taken down

04:24.780 --> 04:29.220
or photographs of the body
in the cell at any time.

04:29.220 --> 04:32.900
There was no description of the
thing that was around his neck

04:32.900 --> 04:38.360
or whatever it was that
he was suspended from

04:38.360 --> 04:42.060
or what he was suspended from.

04:42.060 --> 04:45.620
All we know is that
the wrong ligature

04:45.620 --> 04:50.760
was taken from the scene by
emergency medical technician,

04:50.760 --> 04:54.640
who just saw a noose and put it
onto the gurney with the body.

04:54.640 --> 04:57.300
The one guard who
had found the body

04:57.300 --> 05:00.580
and removed him from the
ligature, the suspension,

05:00.580 --> 05:02.680
refused to be interviewed.

05:02.680 --> 05:05.100
He refused to talk about
what it is that he saw,

05:05.100 --> 05:07.220
so we didn't have
that information.

05:07.220 --> 05:10.120
And when the medical examiner
asked to visit the scene,

05:10.120 --> 05:11.880
she was not allowed to do so.

05:11.880 --> 05:13.720
The New York City
medical examiner

05:13.720 --> 05:16.060
says her analysis was clear cut.

05:16.060 --> 05:20.660
The man killed himself by
hanging or partial hanging,

05:20.660 --> 05:22.680
incomplete hanging.

05:22.680 --> 05:24.582
And you say?

05:24.582 --> 05:26.040
The injuries that
were documented

05:26.040 --> 05:29.240
by the medical examiner
were not consistent

05:29.240 --> 05:32.400
with a suicidal hanging,
and the injuries that

05:32.400 --> 05:35.640
were attributed
to something other

05:35.640 --> 05:40.680
than potentially a physical
struggle or assault were

05:40.680 --> 05:41.900
just overlooked.

05:41.900 --> 05:45.480
Dr. Freeman, is it true that
you've had trouble getting

05:45.480 --> 05:49.520
your work on this published
in peer-reviewed journals?

05:49.520 --> 05:51.920
We came across some very
skeptical peer reviewers who

05:51.920 --> 05:54.520
thought that it was not
appropriate to criticize

05:54.520 --> 05:58.240
a medical examiner's findings
in a peer-reviewed publication.

05:58.240 --> 06:01.160
I've been through this process
a lot of different times,

06:01.160 --> 06:03.860
and sometimes it takes a while
to get the paper published.

06:03.860 --> 06:05.143
This is a big one.

06:05.143 --> 06:07.310
DAVID SCOTT: And this is
where Freeman's work raises

06:07.310 --> 06:09.230
disturbing new questions.

06:09.230 --> 06:11.270
MICHAEL FREEMAN: When
I saw this photograph,

06:11.270 --> 06:17.510
my first reaction was that that
is a very unusual looking injury

06:17.510 --> 06:20.070
for a hanging on the neck.

06:20.070 --> 06:24.470
And the reason I found that
was, one, the injury looks very

06:24.470 --> 06:28.530
horizontal rather than sloped
upwards, in other words,

06:28.530 --> 06:32.670
front to back rather
than sloped upwards.

06:32.670 --> 06:37.910
Now, I see what looks like
two injury patterns, one that

06:37.910 --> 06:43.030
slopes up or peaks, I think
is the terminology, and one

06:43.030 --> 06:44.890
that is more horizontal.

06:47.830 --> 06:50.470
Am I not reading
the picture right?

06:50.470 --> 06:52.530
You're reading
it 100% correct.

06:52.530 --> 06:57.430
You have what looks
like a Y-shaped abrasion

06:57.430 --> 07:01.910
from the application of force
that looks like either force was

07:01.910 --> 07:04.870
applied by something
that had a V in it,

07:04.870 --> 07:10.490
or a force was applied
more than once to the neck.

07:10.490 --> 07:14.370
Suicidal hangings don't
apply force more than once.

07:14.370 --> 07:17.290
There can be a slipping of
the body down a little bit,

07:17.290 --> 07:19.370
but you can't apply
force more than once

07:19.370 --> 07:20.830
in the suicidal hanging.

07:20.830 --> 07:23.050
There is no more
muscular movement

07:23.050 --> 07:28.810
after 10 seconds, 15
seconds, 20 at the outside,

07:28.810 --> 07:30.730
because consciousness
is lost very, very

07:30.730 --> 07:33.290
quickly with suicidal hangings.

07:33.290 --> 07:34.890
DAVID SCOTT: But
the medical examiner

07:34.890 --> 07:39.450
says that the ligature
furrow pattern

07:39.450 --> 07:43.610
is typical of hanging in the
way that it peaks at the side

07:43.610 --> 07:47.970
and doesn't fully surround
the circumference of the neck.

07:47.970 --> 07:52.450
You disagree with both
of those statements?

07:52.450 --> 07:55.650
Well, no, it depends on the
ligature, which we don't have.

07:55.650 --> 07:57.730
Does that mean
it's unknowable?

07:57.730 --> 08:00.970
You can have a
hanging with a ligature

08:00.970 --> 08:02.650
that's tightened to
the back of the neck

08:02.650 --> 08:07.570
with a slipknot that's going to
produce a circumferential injury

08:07.570 --> 08:10.230
pattern all the way around
if the person is completely

08:10.230 --> 08:11.130
suspended.

08:11.130 --> 08:13.030
Or you can have a
partial one, where

08:13.030 --> 08:15.510
there is a point of
suspension that's much higher

08:15.510 --> 08:16.690
and the knot is higher.

08:16.690 --> 08:17.850
It just depends.

08:17.850 --> 08:22.970
We don't even have the
instrument to evaluate that.

08:22.970 --> 08:26.670
So it seems unknowable.

08:26.670 --> 08:27.910
Not completely.

08:27.910 --> 08:31.470
So you will find that typical
full-suspension hanging patterns

08:31.470 --> 08:33.710
are slanted and peaked.

08:33.710 --> 08:37.390
Yet what we find with
Epstein is that they

08:37.390 --> 08:43.590
were neither slanted nor peaked
because we have multiple marks.

08:43.590 --> 08:46.670
So we've got one mark up
here, which is quite faint,

08:46.670 --> 08:48.910
and that is slanted.

08:48.910 --> 08:51.350
Then we have a much
deeper abrasion

08:51.350 --> 08:55.470
which appears contiguous
with that slant,

08:55.470 --> 08:58.710
and that's less slanted.

08:58.710 --> 09:01.130
So that does not look
typical of a hanging.

09:01.130 --> 09:02.765
I'm not saying it
can't be a hanging.

09:02.765 --> 09:04.390
I'm not saying that
the injury couldn't

09:04.390 --> 09:08.550
be produced by somebody
kneeling and leaning forward

09:08.550 --> 09:09.820
into a ligature.

09:09.820 --> 09:11.540
Maybe that happened,
although this

09:11.540 --> 09:15.700
is a very, very deep
ligature for that to happen.

09:15.700 --> 09:18.860
A so-called partial
or incomplete hanging?

09:18.860 --> 09:20.280
MICHAEL FREEMAN: Yes, exactly.

09:20.280 --> 09:22.640
That would be a partial hanging.

09:22.640 --> 09:24.580
This one is more
of a hybrid, where

09:24.580 --> 09:26.480
there is some
support for the feet,

09:26.480 --> 09:28.940
but then most of the
body weight is actually

09:28.940 --> 09:31.580
suspended on the ligature.

09:31.580 --> 09:32.880
So is that possible?

09:32.880 --> 09:33.380
Yes.

09:33.380 --> 09:36.900
Can you produce multiple
force applications

09:36.900 --> 09:41.180
to produce this funny
Y sort of arrangement?

09:41.180 --> 09:43.880
No, that's not going to happen.

09:43.880 --> 09:46.100
Is it possible
that this was more

09:46.100 --> 09:49.380
of a lurching process,
a halting process,

09:49.380 --> 09:53.540
where he made
adjustments to the thing,

09:53.540 --> 09:57.300
where there were perhaps false
starts that might explain

09:57.300 --> 10:01.560
the variation in scarring?

10:01.560 --> 10:04.060
He made a false start
that was strong enough

10:04.060 --> 10:07.220
to break his hyoid bone
and thyroid cartilage

10:07.220 --> 10:11.680
and give him a deep abrasion
and then maintain consciousness

10:11.680 --> 10:14.700
and then did it again does
not actually make sense.

10:14.700 --> 10:16.640
That's not what
suicidal hangings do.

10:16.640 --> 10:19.000
The body becomes
unconscious, and then it

10:19.000 --> 10:21.460
is the weight of the body
that produces the abrasion,

10:21.460 --> 10:24.840
not any kind of
running jump at it.

10:24.840 --> 10:28.520
What you see in this front
view of the ligature pattern

10:28.520 --> 10:31.320
is that it is ragged looking.

10:31.320 --> 10:33.280
It is segmented looking.

10:33.280 --> 10:35.760
It's not smooth.

10:35.760 --> 10:39.760
There's certainly would not be
consistent with the noose that

10:39.760 --> 10:42.320
was taken from the room.

10:42.320 --> 10:45.600
This is the body
face down, looking

10:45.600 --> 10:49.720
at the junction between the
right side of the upper back

10:49.720 --> 10:52.440
and the base of the
neck in the back.

10:52.440 --> 10:53.660
This is an abrasion.

10:53.660 --> 10:56.960
This abrasion happened
at the time of the death.

10:56.960 --> 11:02.080
This abrasion is not
explained by a hanging.

11:02.080 --> 11:06.040
There is no scenario in
which a hanging ligature that

11:06.040 --> 11:10.220
produces the injuries that we
see in the front sloping upward

11:10.220 --> 11:14.110
also produced an injury far
below where the knot or any

11:14.110 --> 11:15.830
of the ligature would be.

11:15.830 --> 11:17.850
This is an unexplained injury.

11:17.850 --> 11:23.550
Wouldn't the most prudent
finding be undetermined?

11:23.550 --> 11:26.187
I think that it would have
been the most prudent finding.

11:26.187 --> 11:28.270
DAVID SCOTT: Freeman bases
some of his assumptions

11:28.270 --> 11:30.950
on the belated interview
federal investigators did

11:30.950 --> 11:34.390
with Michael Thomas, the guard
who purportedly found Epstein

11:34.390 --> 11:35.790
hanging in his cell.

11:35.790 --> 11:39.870
Freeman recreates the scene
to hypothesize about the scars

11:39.870 --> 11:41.750
found on Epstein's body.

11:41.750 --> 11:49.270
In order to accept this new
evidence and its veracity,

11:49.270 --> 11:52.390
we have to believe the
uncorroborated word

11:52.390 --> 11:56.950
of a known liar who is
negotiating with the government

11:56.950 --> 12:01.950
to avoid charges and
probable prison time.

12:01.950 --> 12:07.270
Are you comfortable
relying so heavily

12:07.270 --> 12:14.930
on the statements of a witness
that is himself so tainted?

12:14.930 --> 12:16.450
It's a fair question.

12:16.450 --> 12:20.090
And the answer is,
I don't have to.

12:20.090 --> 12:22.890
I'm proceeding from the idea
that here's the information

12:22.890 --> 12:24.770
that the medical
examiner would have

12:24.770 --> 12:28.250
had because this is all a
medical examiner has to go on.

12:28.250 --> 12:29.810
If they had
interviewed Mr. Thomas

12:29.810 --> 12:33.890
at the time, the information
about how the hanging occurred,

12:33.890 --> 12:35.868
if that information
is what they had,

12:35.868 --> 12:37.410
now they've got to
see whether or not

12:37.410 --> 12:39.650
the autopsy evidence
is consistent

12:39.650 --> 12:41.210
with that information.

12:41.210 --> 12:43.730
That's the operating
principle I'm working under.

12:43.730 --> 12:47.330
This is the view into the
cell from the doorway.

12:47.330 --> 12:52.150
And you can see this bunk
bed at the back of the cell,

12:52.150 --> 12:54.810
but you can't see
the right side of it.

12:54.810 --> 13:01.330
So we have the account from
the other guard, Tova Noel, who

13:01.330 --> 13:03.850
said she heard tearing,
but she didn't see the body

13:03.850 --> 13:05.010
as it was suspended.

13:05.010 --> 13:09.330
Mr. Thomas says he found
the body suspended over

13:09.330 --> 13:16.750
from the right side of the bunk,
which he circled in a photograph

13:16.750 --> 13:18.710
that he was shown
by investigators.

13:18.710 --> 13:22.470
This is his mark on evidence
that was used during the sworn

13:22.470 --> 13:23.390
statement.

13:23.390 --> 13:26.590
He said that the suspension
that he found that Epstein

13:26.590 --> 13:30.150
was hanging from was
not related to this,

13:30.150 --> 13:31.830
what he called a
string, that was

13:31.830 --> 13:35.630
threaded through one of the
holes that was in the bunk bed.

13:35.630 --> 13:38.630
It's not clear how the
suspension would have

13:38.630 --> 13:40.070
been attached to the bunk bed.

13:40.070 --> 13:43.230
I don't have that information.

13:43.230 --> 13:45.590
Also unknowable.

13:45.590 --> 13:48.530
Also unknowable with
any degree of certainty.

13:48.530 --> 13:53.670
How do you explain
the other ligature

13:53.670 --> 13:56.510
furrow, the one above it?

13:56.510 --> 14:00.670
Well, if you start
out strangling somebody

14:00.670 --> 14:04.150
and they lose consciousness
and they slip down

14:04.150 --> 14:06.990
and the hand moves up,
the right hand moves up,

14:06.990 --> 14:10.310
you could have a second contact.

14:10.310 --> 14:12.190
And that second
contact, then, could

14:12.190 --> 14:15.020
continue to apply
pressure until you're

14:15.020 --> 14:19.020
sure you've done the deed
that you're trying to do.

14:19.020 --> 14:20.180
Right.

14:20.180 --> 14:24.940
If you had to set
aside Michael Thomas

14:24.940 --> 14:30.260
and his so-called evidence,
would you still feel that way?

14:30.260 --> 14:31.238
Without question.

14:31.238 --> 14:32.780
DAVID SCOTT: And
Freeman says there's

14:32.780 --> 14:36.460
clear evidence of another severe
injury that was never accounted

14:36.460 --> 14:39.780
for by the medical examiner,
documented in these newly

14:39.780 --> 14:41.860
released autopsy photos.

14:41.860 --> 14:44.900
We warn you, they are
particularly graphic.

14:44.900 --> 14:48.260
So what you're seeing here is
the deltoid muscle, the shoulder

14:48.260 --> 14:49.140
muscle.

14:49.140 --> 14:52.180
And you're seeing this dark red.

14:52.180 --> 14:56.780
That dark red is
hemorrhage into the muscle.

14:56.780 --> 15:02.300
This did not shows a bruise on
the body because the death was

15:02.300 --> 15:06.500
so close to the time that this
happened that no bruise rose.

15:06.500 --> 15:10.140
So when you look at the body,
you don't see any sign of trauma

15:10.140 --> 15:15.100
to the outside because
blood pressure was stopped

15:15.100 --> 15:18.800
almost immediately after
this injury began and began

15:18.800 --> 15:20.180
to hemorrhage into the muscle.

15:20.180 --> 15:22.200
But this is a sign of
blunt force trauma.

15:22.200 --> 15:27.160
You also have injury back
at the top of the neck.

15:27.160 --> 15:29.140
This is described in
the autopsy report.

15:29.140 --> 15:33.860
They actually took a sample
of this little dark area,

15:33.860 --> 15:36.920
which has hemorrhaged into the
muscle at the back of the head.

15:36.920 --> 15:39.360
I don't put a lot
of stock in that.

15:39.360 --> 15:42.560
You might find
that in a hanging.

15:42.560 --> 15:47.160
You're not going to find a
sign of blunt force trauma

15:47.160 --> 15:49.120
in a suicidal hanging,
unless there's

15:49.120 --> 15:51.880
other evidence of why you
would have that injury

15:51.880 --> 15:53.660
right around the time of death.

15:53.660 --> 15:57.760
Is it possible that these
were pre-existing injuries?

15:57.760 --> 16:01.222
Well, it's not very possible
that the deltoid injury was

16:01.222 --> 16:03.180
a pre-existing injury
without raising a bruise,

16:03.180 --> 16:04.900
which would have come
within hours of it.

16:04.900 --> 16:07.600
This is definitely
perimortem, meaning right

16:07.600 --> 16:08.800
around the time of death.

16:08.800 --> 16:11.800
Given how little
reliable data is

16:11.800 --> 16:17.870
available from the death slash
crime scene and circumstances,

16:17.870 --> 16:19.410
how sure are you?

16:19.410 --> 16:23.710
How can you be so sure?

16:23.710 --> 16:28.190
What I'm sure is that these
injuries are not explained

16:28.190 --> 16:29.450
by a suicidal hanging.

16:32.750 --> 16:34.630
What produced the injuries?

16:34.630 --> 16:36.210
I can't be certain about it.

16:36.210 --> 16:40.110
I can say this is reasonable
because the death's not

16:40.110 --> 16:41.310
a natural death.

16:41.310 --> 16:44.110
He didn't die from
disease process.

16:44.110 --> 16:49.110
So really, if you rule out
suicide, all that's left

16:49.110 --> 16:50.770
is a homicide of some kind.

16:50.770 --> 16:54.430
Do you concede that in
the totality of the case

16:54.430 --> 17:01.295
and what we know that
suicide is still plausible?

17:01.295 --> 17:02.670
You're going to
have to come up

17:02.670 --> 17:06.270
with an explanation for how
we got injuries that did not

17:06.270 --> 17:08.869
result from a hanging.

17:08.869 --> 17:12.069
Freeman's work raises
disturbing new questions.

17:12.069 --> 17:15.650
Whether he also answers
them is less evident.

17:15.650 --> 17:19.170
My conjecture in trying to
fill in the missing pieces

17:19.170 --> 17:24.490
has to do with how might a
homicide have been accomplished.

17:24.490 --> 17:27.970
But I'm not conjecturing
about these injuries could not

17:27.970 --> 17:30.490
be explained by a
suicidal hanging.

17:30.490 --> 17:32.090
That's not conjecture.

17:32.090 --> 17:35.770
<i>The New York Times</i>
drew a conclusion

17:35.770 --> 17:37.990
that it was a suicide.

17:37.990 --> 17:39.550
You've seen their reasoning.

17:39.550 --> 17:41.370
What did you make of it?

17:41.370 --> 17:43.090
Well, it wasn't
reasoning that included

17:43.090 --> 17:45.050
an evaluation of the
autopsy file, which

17:45.050 --> 17:47.990
only myself and my colleagues
have been able to do.

17:47.990 --> 17:49.850
Mark Epstein
thinks this is going

17:49.850 --> 17:54.890
to change everything and reopen
the investigation possibly.

17:54.890 --> 17:57.030
Do you?

17:57.030 --> 17:57.530
I don't.

17:57.530 --> 18:01.290
I think that the scientific
world and the general public

18:01.290 --> 18:04.850
will hear about this
analysis, and they

18:04.850 --> 18:06.250
can make up their own minds.

18:06.250 --> 18:09.610
We are releasing the entire
file from the autopsy

18:09.610 --> 18:14.410
to the general public so
that there will no longer be

18:14.410 --> 18:20.240
a hoarding of
information and everyone

18:20.240 --> 18:22.120
can draw their own conclusions.

18:22.120 --> 18:25.520
In fact, neither the
medical examiner nor Freeman

18:25.520 --> 18:28.080
can be proven right
or wrong, at least

18:28.080 --> 18:31.760
not until the yawning gaps in
the investigation are filled.

18:31.760 --> 18:34.700
We reached out to the New York
City medical examiner who said,

18:34.700 --> 18:38.463
quote, "our office stands
by its determination."

18:38.463 --> 18:39.880
This is one of
these cases where

18:39.880 --> 18:43.000
everybody's got an opinion.

18:43.000 --> 18:46.880
And I'm trying to
show that there

18:46.880 --> 18:51.880
can be greater basis for an
opinion than just saying,

18:51.880 --> 18:54.280
I think it all
looks a bit fishy.

18:54.280 --> 18:54.860
It does.

18:54.860 --> 18:57.040
It can look fishy
as hell, but that

18:57.040 --> 18:59.320
doesn't mean it's a homicide.

18:59.320 --> 19:04.680
But if we can show that it
really couldn't be a suicide,

19:04.680 --> 19:08.000
then we have to start
thinking toward homicide.

19:08.000 --> 19:12.720
I'm not going to chalk it
up as a monolithic mystery

19:12.720 --> 19:15.480
that no one will ever
know the answer to.

19:15.480 --> 19:19.710
I think there's some things we
do have better answers to now.
